WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.020
She's not a bird or a plane, and she's definitely
not Superman!

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.920
Melissa Benoist is CW’s Supergirl, but how
did she get ready for the part?

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:12.139
Let’s take a look at how this self-proclaimed
musical theater nerd got into fighting condition

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.980
to play the high flying, hard punching Supergirl.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.640
You don't just get handed a role like Supergirl.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:22.100
Melissa Benoist had to go through a rigorous
audition process before she got anywhere close

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:23.200
to landing the role.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:27.460
The wait was made even more excruciating because,
according to Benoist, she was the among the

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.460
first actors to audition for the part.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:34.360
That first audition was in October 2014, and
it was just the start of what would become

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:35.600
a months-long process.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:40.600
She told Entertainment Weekly that she didn't
learn she landed the part until February 2015.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.080
In the interim, she said,

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.360
"I went through multiple screen-tests, multiple
auditions with the producing team.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:47.560
There was a lot."

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:52.280
During that extensive audition process, Variety
reported that Benoist found a champion in

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:53.800
producer Greg Berlanti.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:54.900
Benoist said,

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.940
"Greg championed me the whole time and was
in my corner.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.540
Even when I didn't think the part was mine,
he was always rooting for me.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.100
That support goes a long way, especially when
I'm fighting for something I want so badly.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:07.920
His belief in me really touched me."

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:12.020
Berlanti echoed the sentiment, praising the
Supergirl star by saying,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.040
"If we had not found her, I would have said,
'I don't want to make this.'"

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:20.400
The first episode aired almost a year after
that first audition, on October 26, 2015.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.800
She's inhabited the role ever since, serving
as an inspiration for fans around the world.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.900
"Are there like a million little girls who
dress up as Supergirl now, as a result of

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:30.540
the show?"

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:33.200
"Every Halloween there are quite a bit of
Supergirls."

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.260
One thing that all superheroes have in common
are incredible abs, and Benoist's Supergirl

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:39.140
is no exception.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:43.140
She has said in interviews that to prepare
for the role of Kara Zor-El , she focused

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:48.620
on both strength training and core work, getting
toned with a combination of Pilates, boxing,

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:49.540
and plyometrics.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.820
If you've never had a reason to get into superhero
shape, you might be wondering what plyometrics

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:54.320
are.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:58.820
Plyometrics involve exerting your body to
its maximum ability for a short period of

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:02.460
time for example, by jumping to improve strength,
speed, and power.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:06.100
As Benoist found out, plyo routines are far
from easy work.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:08.440
"I did quite a bit of training.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:11.450
We did this stuff called plyometrics, which
is like jump training.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:15.140
And all of it was really hard — just hard
things!"

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:19.180
All that focus on the core isn't just for
the sake of looking good.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.620
It also comes in handy when you're doing flying
stunts.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:28.960
"I'm up on a wire, and you have to hold your
entire body weight so there's a lot of training

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:29.460
involved."

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:34.100
Melissa Benoist wasn't the only cast member
who had to up her workout intensity to give

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:36.300
the action sequences the effort they deserved.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.980
Chyler Leigh, who plays Kara's adoptive sister
Alex, said that the actors work really hard

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.980
with the stunt coordinators to get the fight
scenes just right.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:47.180
And those action-packed days are full-on workouts
in their own right.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:48.560
She told CBS' Watch!

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:49.180
Magazine,

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:53.440
"When we do fight scenes, we're working out
for six hours, just doing the same thing.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:55.280
We are burnt for the next few days.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:57.940
I think that's really where I get my caloric
burn count."

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:01.840
Even when they aren't shooting, Leigh and
Benoist try to use those periods of waiting

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.720
around that happen on the set of a TV show
to stay in shape.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:05.680
She said,

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.440
"Melissa and I try to do as much as we possibly
can.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.490
In my trailer, I've got resistance bands and
medicine balls and things like that.

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:15.180
I try to do it in between, but we have very
little downtime."

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:19.420
The two on-screen sisters also have a shared
interest in pilates, which Leigh says she

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:21.220
fits into her schedule when she can.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:25.260
And you thought TV sets were all hanging out
in trailers, enjoying craft services.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:26.840
"YES!"

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:32.500
Another crucial element of being a superhero
is being able to convincingly kick some villainous

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:33.000
butt.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.900
This didn't come naturally to Benoist, who
confessed to the Chicago Tribune,

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:38.700
"I had never punched anyone!

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:43.300
I maybe slapped my sisters when I was a kid,
but I'm not an aggressive, violent person,

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.280
and I had no idea how to throw a punch."

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:49.360
In order to help herself find her inner fighter,
Benoist took up boxing.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:50.520
She told InStyle,

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:52.320
"I definitely had to learn technique.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.580
Like how to actually punch someone and not
hurt yourself more than you're hurting the

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:56.800
person you're punching."

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:59.900
Fortunately for Benoist, she had access to
excellent tutors.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:04.599
In addition to the stunt team, former karate
and kickboxing world champion Lexi Alexander

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.420
directed an episode in the first season.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:10.920
Her directing stint was short, but Alexander
told Den of Geek that she pushed her star

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.200
to the limit, saying:

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:16.199
"This was a brand new thing for everybody
involved, and everybody was excited that a

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:19.860
real fighter was getting Melissa [Benoist]
to the next level of fight choreography."

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:24.360
In fact, the change in Benoist's technique
was so dramatic that Alexander said the series'

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:28.820
stunt team changed Benoist's fighting style
going forward to incorporate her expert tips.

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:32.760
So while Benoist might at heart still be more
a lover than a fighter, we still wouldn't

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:34.840
want to challenge her to throw down at this
point.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:36.440
"I actually kind of love it.“

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:41.300
Believe it or not, it really is Benoist doing
some of those hair-raising stunts on the show.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.980
The actress told Variety that she likes to
get involved with the action when she can,

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:45.480
saying,

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.980
"I have been doing a lot [of stunts], and
I want to keep doing them.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:50.320
[...] Supergirl's fight moves are boxing.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:51.760
She's really heavy-handed.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:55.740
There's some flying that involves kicking
and punching mid-flight that's kind of awesome."

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:59.520
However, not everyone was thrilled with Benoist's
hands-on approach to fights.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:03.560
Her mom, Julie, told her that she found it
difficult to watch her daughter's fight scenes,

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:04.780
especially against men.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.760
But Julie Benoist needn't have worried.

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:10.680
Melissa knows her limits, and she's also heaped
praise on her stunt doubles.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:14.120
In 2015, she spoke to InStyle on the subject
of stunts, saying,

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:15.740
"I think they're so fun.

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:18.260
There are some that I just can't I really
could get injured."

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.580
"You should rest some more."

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:22.160
"Since when has that ever worked with me?

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:22.980
Heheh."

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.480
Glee was not Benoist's first experience with
musical theater.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:30.699
She says she's been practicing the craft since
she was four years old, attending classes

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:32.780
in tap, ballet, and jazz dance.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.860
But she wasn't always a perfect student, especially
in ballet.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:39.775
"All the years I took ballet class [teeny
splice here] I was the one kid in class who

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:43.620
the teacher was always like, 'Urgh, her turn-out,'
and, 'She is not standing up straight.'"

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:47.500
She did better with another form of dance,
which she showed off in a video for Glamour.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:52.600
"I have a secret superpower — that I can
tap dance."

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:00.020
That dance training comes in handy on the
set of Supergirl and not just for Benoist's

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:02.419
part in the musical crossover episode of The
Flash.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:07.940
"I have to say, I'm not impressed / By your
more famous cousin."

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:09.120
"Thank you!

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:10.380
No one ever says that."

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:14.020
Benoist said that her dance background helps
her with the wire work and even the fight

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:14.740
sequences.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:16.300
She told the Chicago Tribune,

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:20.199
"The wire work and flying, even the choreography
[...] kind of came like it would if I were

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:21.420
learning a dance routine."

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:24.960
It's also a handy skill that keeps her and
the crew entertained between takes.

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:29.780
Melissa Benoist's favorite thing about her
superhero role is exactly what you'd expect:

00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:31.040
the chance to fly.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:33.900
"It seems like flying wouldn't be so great
when you're pretending to fly."

00:06:33.909 --> 00:06:34.909
"I mean, I get to fly!

00:06:34.909 --> 00:06:38.260
I mean it's hard work, I'm essentially attached
to a fork in the air."

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:43.419
Despite how some skilled animators make it
look on screen, flying isn't easy, especially

00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:45.240
when you have to act like it's all effortless
to you.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:48.720
Benoist told Variety just how hard it was
and how she prepared, saying,

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:51.300
"The wire work for training is mostly core
work.

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:55.780
It's mostly ab-centric — the whole area
of the body that nobody wants to work out.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:57.160
You have to get strong.

00:06:57.160 --> 00:06:59.599
You have to carry your whole body weight when
you're up in the air."

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:03.120
However, it's worth all the working out to
make the process run smoothly.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:04.220
She told People,

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:06.960
"The wire work is really difficult, but so
fun.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:09.780
And when you get it right, watching the result
is exhilarating.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.259
It's a really, really cool feeling to know
what it felt like, the energy I had to exert

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:15.640
to create those flying scenes.

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:18.020
And then the way they look is really rewarding."

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.500
Benoist got some help perfecting her flying
technique.

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:24.560
When Jimmy Kimmel asked how she figured out
Supergirl's flying style, she had a surprising

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:25.500
answer in store.

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:31.520
"There's this sort of superhero physicality
school that you kind of have to go to

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:35.780
where the
stunt team will teach you to walk like a superhero…"

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:37.880
And that's how to look fly in spandex.

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:41.720
All of that working out and on-set action
builds up an appetite.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:45.880
But as a female superhero who's expected to
squeeze into a tight superhero suit every

00:07:45.889 --> 00:07:49.990
day, and have the muscles to beat up bad guys
without actually looking all that bulked up,

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:52.740
Benoist can't exactly go to town on craft
services.

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:55.560
Fortunately, she's pretty into fruits and
vegetables anyway.

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:56.560
She gushed to Glamour,

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:02.559
"I love supermarkets, I love grocery shopping,
I love the produce aisle, because to be super

00:08:02.559 --> 00:08:03.999
you have to be healthy.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:06.940
And I would probably stock up on all the fruits.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:10.600
However, she isn't about to totally deprive
herself of comfort foods.

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:12.500
In the same Glamour interview, she said,

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:17.540
"If I could supersize any food it would be
[an] ice cream Drumstick, vanilla caramel.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:19.800
They're so good."

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:24.860
In 2016, she told the Chicago Tribune that
she was excited to attend that year's Chicago

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:30.449
Comic-Con and Entertainment Expo, aka C2E2,
not just to commune with fellow artists and

00:08:30.449 --> 00:08:33.100
fans, but because she wanted to check out
the city's restaurants.

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:37.580
As she explained, quote, "I've heard the food
scene is incredible, and I'm a foodie, so

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:38.880
I can't wait to go out and eat."

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:43.620
Benoist also said that her own partiality
to the occasional junk food splurge inspired

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:46.580
the writers to add the habit into her character's
personality.

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:47.440
She said,

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:51.480
"I love junk food, I love doughnuts, and they
put that in there quite a bit."

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:53.240
Do you like donuts?"

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:54.700
"Who doesn't like donuts."

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:55.940
"I know a place."

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:59.370
It just goes to show that as long as you're
disciplined with your workouts, you don't

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:01.540
have to deprive yourself of every treat you
love.

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:05.800
One reason Melissa Benoist was so invested
in making sure her character could be physically

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:09.550
impressive on screen was because she wanted
to inspire other women to feel strong and

00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:10.840
powerful in their own lives.

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:12.580
In an essay for Time, she wrote,

00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:16.280
"I think the more the show goes on, the more
you see this fierceness and what women are

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:17.240
really capable of.

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:19.800
It puts [Supergirl] on this equal playing
field.

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:23.680
[…] That's so meaningful to me to help children
understand that girls can have just as much

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:26.320
fun as their male counterparts in the superhero
world.

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:30.080
It's about confidence and hope, and Supergirl
really embodies all of that."

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:34.920
Even though Benoist also wrote that she grew
up in a, quote, "really strong, feminine family,"

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:36.340
she told the Chicago Tribune,

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:39.840
"I didn't really believe that I could be that
character or play a superhero.

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:43.500
It's something I never thought of myself as
doing, but I just knew I had to go for it

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:47.380
because there's so few female superheroes
and just strong females that fight back and

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:49.220
do it with grace and do it with love."

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:53.720
She's also said that she didn't want the character
to be a female version of male superheroes,

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:54.220
saying,

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:57.920
"What I think is cool is to try to bring kind
of a femininity to her strength.

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:00.610
[…] I do like the idea of bringing in like
a grace to it.

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:03.160
And a fluidity and kind of this femininity
to flying.

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:04.060
It's fun."

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:08.420
In other words, Melissa Benoist is out here
bringing new meaning to (Super) girl power.

